; aiasync.eval - the AI workers' lifetime, on the WALL CLOCK (code-review batch
; 34: C52, C69). tools\AITest.py runs this and JUDGES it; on its own it only
; reports. Every section header is `=== <name> ===`, as in ai.eval.
;
; NO `reset` AND NO LOCKSTEP, and both are the point. Lockstep pauses the bucket
; workers, and `reset` recycles the world down a path no player takes - so the
; harness never saw a worker keep thinking from the last fight while the world
; stood frozen on the title. A real new game opens the starter dungeon (crypt1,
; 7,7), the bucket workers think on the wall clock, and `aiwait` blocks until
; every one of them has published twice more, so the thinking this is about
; has certainly happened before the next line looks.
;
; MUST RUN IN ITS OWN PROCESS, first: the opening `newgame` is a cold start, and
; a `reset` before it would send every new game to the harness's own ground.
; And with `-project dungeon-demo` (AITest passes it): without it the world
; switch at the end saves Test-World in settings.ini as the last world played,
; which the next ordinary launch would open.
logecho on
newgame
lockstep off
lockstep
seed 11
pos
monsters
; A save from before any fight: every monster unaware. The title's Continue
; loads the newest save through LoadGame, which is what `load` calls.
save aitest_async

; --- C52: Continue after a fight ------------------------------------------------
; Maren's Fire Bolt wakes the skeleton behind the brazier (ai.eval's brazier
; section): it hunts the party, and every snapshot says so. The workers think
; from that, the party leaves for the title - where nothing is published, and
; the workers go on thinking from the last snapshot, a fresh batch each tick -
; and then Continue. The first frame back used to apply those batches and latch
; `aware` again for every monster that was hunting.
echo === continue ===
tp 8 4
face e
learn 2 fire
learn 2 project
setskill 2 fire 5
tally reset
cast 2 0 fire project
step 5
tally
monsters
aiwait
title
state
aiwait
load aitest_async
step 0.5
pos
monsters

; --- C52: Start New Game after a fight ------------------------------------------
; The same fight, then the title's Start New Game. With the dungeon resident and
; the starter level the one in memory, the new game resets the world IN PLACE
; (`New game started in crypt (crypt1 at 7,7)` in the log, no level load) -
; which is the case that kept the monsters and their old ids.
echo === newgame ===
tp 8 4
face e
learn 2 fire
learn 2 project
setskill 2 fire 5
tally reset
cast 2 0 fire project
step 5
tally
monsters
aiwait
title
state
aiwait
newgame
step 0.5
pos
monsters

; --- C69: a world switch takes its AI workers with it ---------------------------
; Switching worlds destroys this world's AI director. Its four workers used to
; be STOPPED, leaving four Dead `ai.bucketN` slots that still held jobs aimed
; at the destroyed director - a `boot` on one ran it against freed memory.
; After the switch there must be exactly the new director's four, all alive,
; under ids the old four did not have (ids are never reused).
echo === worlds ===
threads
worlds load Test-World
state
threads
echo === end ===
